The command group is the one-release `primary-goal-v1` compatibility adapter.

```bash
# Create only when no primary goal is active.
task-board goal set-primary \
  --objective "Keep the parent goal aligned with the current user requirements." \
  --reason "First actionable requirement"

# CAS-replace the whole objective. Repeating the same objective is idempotent.
task-board goal update --if-revision 1 \
  --objective "Keep the parent goal aligned with the current user requirements and active delivery work." \
  --reason "Requirement expanded"

# Read current state, immutable audit history, or one immutable revision.
task-board goal get
task-board goal get --history
task-board goal get --goal-id PRIMARY-GOAL-260723-a1b2c3 --revision 1

# Clear only with the observed revision and an explicit disposition reason.
task-board goal clear --if-revision 2 --reason "Parent intent superseded"
```

`goal get` is read-only and remains available inside a spawned run. When
`TASK_BOARD_RUN_ID` is set, `set-primary`, `update`, and `clear` return the
typed `primary_goal_parent_context_required` failure with no side effects;
spawned owners must use `task-board spawn goal` or `task-board spawn goal
upsert` for their `run/RUN-*` owner. Both commands use `board-goal-v2`; owner
isolation prevents either compatibility adapter from silently updating or
clearing the other.

Every successful JSON response has `ok`, `board_scope`, `primary_goal`, and
`latest_ref`; mutations additionally carry `mutation`. Exact revision reads
add `primary_goal_revision`, and history reads add newest-first `history`
summaries. Typed failures use
`{ok:false,error:{code,message,current_ref,side_effects:false,remediation?}}`.
Remote primary-adapter mutations accept authoritative `goal=board-v2` or the
one-release `primary_goal=board-v1` compatibility capability. Direct v2
mutations require `goal=board-v2`, and reads never create a local shadow
ledger.

## Decision table

| Need | Tool | Example |
|------|------|---------|
| Discover full API contract | DSL | `task-board q 'schema()'` |
| Repair one operation/mutation call | DSL | `task-board q 'schema(operation=NAME)'` / `schema(mutation=NAME)` |
| Preflight one spawn target | DSL | `task-board q 'project_config(view=spawn-preflight, role=ROLE, agent=PROVIDER)'` |
| Structured query (status, filter, lookup) | DSL query | `task-board q --format compact 'list(type=task, status=development) { overview }'` |
| Structured write (status, assign, link, create) | DSL mutation | `task-board m 'set_status(TASK-XX, status=development)'` |
| Evidence-backed producer handoff | CLI facade over shared mutation | `task-board handoff TASK-XX --role developer` |
| Board parent intent | CLI primary-goal group | `task-board goal get` / `task-board goal update --if-revision N ...` |
| Preview write without applying | DSL mutation | `task-board m --dry-run 'delete(TASK-XX)'` |
| Text search across elements | Grep | `task-board grep "AudioRecorder"` |
| **Phase planning (canonical API)** | **DSL query** | **`task-board q 'plan(EPIC-XX, mode=children)'`** |
| **Pretty-print / render / save a plan** | **CLI facade** | **`task-board plan EPIC-XX --render --format png`** |
| Resource management | DSL mutation / CLI | `task-board m 'add_resource(TASK-XX, name=TASK-XX_results.md, content="...", type=outcome)'` |

**IMPORTANT:** Canonical planning lives in `task-board q 'plan(...)'`. Use `task-board plan` only as a local pretty-print / render / save facade over that same planning model. Never manually create phase breakdowns or write `.planning/` files from scratch.

