# TASK-260715-ypo7yo — handoff evidence, round 5 (verdict-04 rework)

Revision **2026-07-28.r5**. Status: **ready for review**. No portal mutation has
occurred and none is authorized by this handoff.

Verdict 04 raised three blocking findings, all three characterised as recoverable
architecture/metadata corrections needing no human decision. That is how they were
handled. **All three are CONFIRMED and closed.** Two are contract changes, one is
board metadata.

## 1. F1 — the macOS host Keychain group has no sharing consumer

**CONFIRMED.** r4 authored `keychain-access-groups` as `required` on `macos.host`
while its own rationale said the macOS provider never reads the group. The
reviewer's diagnosis is exactly right, and sharper than "an unused entitlement":
r4 had applied the least-privilege test to the **provider** row (D-2, rule K1) and
never to the **host** row, because K1 is written about the root/system-domain
provider and is silent on the host.

**Withheld.** `keychain-access-groups` is now `prohibited` on `macos.host`, and
therefore on all four macOS targets. New **rule K2** (§4.4, **D-8**) states the
test K1 did not cover.

### Why this is safe, verified rather than assumed

The load-bearing question is whether the host can still reach its own vault. It
can, and Apple documents it. The two pages the verdict itself cited render via
JavaScript, so their documentation JSON endpoints were read instead:

> "Xcode automatically adds the `application-identifier` entitlement (or the
> `com.apple.application-identifier` entitlement in macOS) to every app during
> code signing, formed as the team identifier (team ID) plus the bundle
> identifier (bundle ID)."
>
> "The system recognizes this app ID as the name of your app's default keychain
> access group … Any keychain items stored with this access group are **private
> to App One**."
>
> "If you don't specify any keychain access groups, then **the app ID is the
> default**."
>
> — *Sharing access to keychain items among a collection of apps*

> "macOS builds the list of data protection keychain access groups available to
> your program from its code signing entitlements. These entitlements must be
> authorized by a provisioning profile."
>
> — TN3137

`macos.host` is provisioned on both channels (**R14**), so its injected
`com.apple.application-identifier` is profile-authorized and the default group is
available. The host's vault and the pre-seed staging item are both host-only, so
both are served by it. The reviewer's second exit — name a second macOS consumer —
was checked and is empty: K1 excludes the root/system-domain provider, and both
macOS probe rows already withheld the key.

### One thing the verdict did not name, found by taking it seriously

The same source shows the entitlement array is ordered `keychain-access-groups`
first, then the app identifier — **the first element is the default**. So
withdrawing the entitlement does not merely remove sharing, it **moves the
default group**:

| | r4 default on `macos.host` | r5 default on `macos.host` |
| --- | --- | --- |
| | `262RZ595FP.works.relux.tunnel.shared` | `262RZ595FP.works.relux.tunnel.mac` |

`TASK-260715-379cpk` must therefore not pass `kSecAttrAccessGroup` on macOS —
naming the shared group now returns `errSecMissingEntitlement` — while iOS keeps
naming it explicitly. A platform branch in the vault, not a shared constant. No
item has been written yet, so there is nothing to migrate; recorded as a
downstream requirement rather than left to surface at first build.

## 2. F3 — the iOS probe App Group grant is not traced to the probe contract

**CONFIRMED.** `TASK-260715-1jckn0` was read in full. Its scope is
`NETunnelProviderManager` save/load/enable/start/status/app-message/stop, one
versioned provider response, and clean stop; **AC3** puts the whole host↔provider
exchange on app messaging. No shared container, `UserDefaults` suite, or snapshot
appears anywhere. r4's claim that the probe "exercises the same shared-container
path production depends on" is unsupported by the probe's own contract.

**Withheld** on both iOS probe rows (§4.3, **D-9**). Rule G4 gained an explicit
**`probeRule`**: a probe inherits **identifiers and profile classes**, never
entitlements; each probe row is tested against its own probe task. If a probe is
meant to prove a capability can be *provisioned*, that is a verification
deliverable and must be an acceptance criterion first — the entitlement follows
the criterion, not the reverse.

The reviewer's alternative exit — amend the probe task to add a shared-container
deliverable — was **considered and declined**: no board element asks for one, and
adding a deliverable purely to justify an already-granted entitlement is the
circularity the finding names. That exit stays open as the rows' `reopensOnly`.

### Consequence: a record is deleted, not deferred

`group.works.relux.tunnel.probe` had exactly two consumers and both just lost the
grant. A portal record no target reads is surface with no reader, so **the record
is deleted**. **Rule G3** is restated as the stronger property it now has: *no
probe carries an App Group at all*, on either platform — disposability by
construction rather than by careful naming. One App Group record remains.

