revisionLog.0.summary	Two least-privilege corrections and one board-metadata correction. F1: keychain-access-groups is now PROHIBITED on macos.host and therefore on all four macOS targets. New rule K2 states the test K1 did not cover - the entitlement's only function is sharing, and the macOS host has no second member to share with, because rule K1 excludes the root/system-domain provider and neither probe touches the Data Protection Keychain. The host keeps full access to its own items through the default access group the injected com.apple.application-identifier provides. K2 also records the ordering law that makes withholding safe and its one real consequence: the macOS default group moves from the shared literal to the app-ID literal, which TASK-260715-379cpk must be authored against. F3: com.apple.security.application-groups is now PROHIBITED on ios.probe.host and ios.probe.provider, because TASK-260715-1jckn0 scopes the probe to the NETunnelProviderManager lifecycle and versioned app messaging and names no shared container; G4 gains an explicit probeRule saying a probe inherits identifiers and profile classes but never entitlements. That left group.works.relux.tunnel.probe with no consumer, so the record is DELETED and G3 is restated as the stronger property that no probe carries a group at all. One App Group record remains. F2: TASK-260728-q5kjta's scope and AC4 were corrected on the board to name the same four macOS App IDs, Network Extensions only, zero App Group records and capabilities, no Keychain portal mutation, the Mac Development profile class, and all four excluded iOS App IDs. Every r2/r3/r4 decision the reviewer listed as acceptable is preserved.
revisionLog.3.summary	Network Extension values are now modelled per signing channel; App Group identifiers moved to the iOS style on both platforms; the blanket 'no key outside the matrix union' assertion was replaced with explicit authored/signing-generated allowlists; App Sandbox rationale is attributed per requirement source. Identity, legacy, probe, environment, and dependency decisions from r1 are preserved unchanged.
appGroupPurposeRule.iOSProbe	Not granted. r5, on reviewer verdict 04 F3: TASK-260715-1jckn0 scopes the disposable probe to the NETunnelProviderManager lifecycle and versioned app messaging and names no shared container, so under G4 the probe has no App Group function of its own. Withholding it left the probe App Group record with no consumer, so the record is deleted too (rule G3) - a portal record no target reads is surface without a function.
appGroupDisjointnessRule.statement	No probe target carries an App Group at all, on either platform. This is strictly stronger than the r4 property that the production and probe records were disjoint: with no probe grant there is no probe state that could reach production state, so the probe stays genuinely disposable by construction rather than by careful naming.
appGroupDisjointnessRule.history	r1-r4 declared a second record, group.works.relux.tunnel.probe, disjoint from production's and consumed by ios.probe.host and ios.probe.provider. r5 withheld the entitlement from both under rule G4 (reviewer verdict 04 F3), which left that record with no consumer, so it is deleted rather than carried as deferred portal surface.
appGroupDisjointnessRule.ifReintroduced	If a probe target ever regains an App Group under G4's reopening condition, its record MUST be a distinct iOS-style 'group.' literal disjoint from every production record, and this rule reverts to a disjointness test over two records.
targets.0.entitlements.keychain-access-groups.rationale	Withheld under least privilege (rule K2). keychain-access-groups is a SHARING construct, and on macOS this contract has no second member to share with. The host's own Data Protection Keychain vault (TASK-260715-379cpk) does not need it: the com.apple.application-identifier entitlement that the signing toolchain injects into every provisioned macOS target is itself an access group, items stored in it are private to the app, and it becomes the default access group when no keychain-access-groups entitlement is present. The macOS provider cannot be the second member - it runs as root against the file-based system-domain keychain, which has no access groups at all (rule K1) - and neither probe target touches the Data Protection Keychain. The r4 rationale rested on a host-owned pre-seed staging item held across system-extension approval; that item is written and read by the host alone, so the default group serves it. Persistence across an interrupted approval flow is a property of the keychain, not of a shared access group.
targets.4.entitlements.keychain-access-groups.reopensOnly	This row reopens if, and only if, TASK-260715-1r0fxv gains a deliverable that stores a credential the probe must read back, AND a second macOS probe target must read the same item. Neither holds: the macOS probe exercises the NETunnelProviderManager lifecycle and a versioned app message, it stores no credential, and rule K1 excludes its root-context provider from Data Protection Keychain membership in any case. It does NOT reopen so that the probe can rehearse the production keychain shape - production carries no macOS keychain group either after rule K2, so there is no shape to rehearse.
targets.5.entitlements.keychain-access-groups.reopensOnly	This row reopens if, and only if, TASK-260715-1r0fxv gains a deliverable that stores a credential the probe must read back, AND a second macOS probe target must read the same item. Neither holds: the macOS probe exercises the NETunnelProviderManager lifecycle and a versioned app message, it stores no credential, and rule K1 excludes its root-context provider from Data Protection Keychain membership in any case. It does NOT reopen so that the probe can rehearse the production keychain shape - production carries no macOS keychain group either after rule K2, so there is no shape to rehearse.
targets.6.entitlements.com.apple.security.application-groups.rationale	Withheld under rule G4: the probe's own contract names no shared-container function. TASK-260715-1jckn0 scopes the disposable iOS probe to NETunnelProviderManager save, load, enable, start, status, app-message and stop, one provider protocol and a versioned response, and a clean stop; its AC3 puts the entire host-to-provider exchange on app messaging, which needs no App Group. The r4 rationale said the probe 'exercises the same shared-container path production depends on', but the path the probe actually exercises is sendProviderMessage, and G4 tests a mechanism currently selected on THIS target, never one inherited from the target it resembles. Granting it would make a disposable probe carry an entitlement it never calls and would put an App Group record into circulation with no reader.
targets.6.entitlements.com.apple.security.application-groups.reopensOnly	This row reopens if, and only if, TASK-260715-1jckn0 gains a concrete shared-container deliverable - a named snapshot or UserDefaults exchange that the probe writes on one side and reads on the other - carrying its own acceptance criterion and its own archive or runtime evidence. It does NOT reopen because the production iOS rows carry the group, nor to keep the probe target shaped like production, nor because iOS re-arms under ADR-024. Reopening is an amendment under section 11 that names the new probe acceptance criterion and re-creates the probe App Group record deleted in r5.
targets.6.entitlements.keychain-access-groups.reopensOnly	This row reopens if, and only if, TASK-260715-1jckn0 gains a deliverable in which the probe host writes a credential that the probe appex must read back, with its own acceptance criterion and evidence. Today the probe stores no credential: its scope is the NETunnelProviderManager lifecycle and a versioned app message, and its provider protocol carries no secret. It does NOT reopen merely because the production iOS pair shares a group - under rule G4's probeRule a probe inherits identifiers and profile classes, never entitlements.
targets.7.entitlements.com.apple.security.application-groups.rationale	Withheld under rule G4: the probe's own contract names no shared-container function. TASK-260715-1jckn0 scopes the disposable iOS probe to NETunnelProviderManager save, load, enable, start, status, app-message and stop, one provider protocol and a versioned response, and a clean stop; its AC3 puts the entire host-to-provider exchange on app messaging, which needs no App Group. The r4 rationale said the probe 'exercises the same shared-container path production depends on', but the path the probe actually exercises is sendProviderMessage, and G4 tests a mechanism currently selected on THIS target, never one inherited from the target it resembles. Granting it would make a disposable probe carry an entitlement it never calls and would put an App Group record into circulation with no reader.
targets.7.entitlements.com.apple.security.application-groups.reopensOnly	This row reopens if, and only if, TASK-260715-1jckn0 gains a concrete shared-container deliverable - a named snapshot or UserDefaults exchange that the probe writes on one side and reads on the other - carrying its own acceptance criterion and its own archive or runtime evidence. It does NOT reopen because the production iOS rows carry the group, nor to keep the probe target shaped like production, nor because iOS re-arms under ADR-024. Reopening is an amendment under section 11 that names the new probe acceptance criterion and re-creates the probe App Group record deleted in r5.
targets.7.entitlements.keychain-access-groups.reopensOnly	This row reopens if, and only if, TASK-260715-1jckn0 gains a deliverable in which the probe host writes a credential that the probe appex must read back, with its own acceptance criterion and evidence. Today the probe stores no credential: its scope is the NETunnelProviderManager lifecycle and a versioned app message, and its provider protocol carries no secret. It does NOT reopen merely because the production iOS pair shares a group - under rule G4's probeRule a probe inherits identifiers and profile classes, never entitlements.
verification.assertions.5.text	LEAST PRIVILEGE (rule G4) — com.apple.security.application-groups is ABSENT from every macOS bundle on every channel, production and probe alike, and no macOS provisioning profile's Entitlements dict carries the key or names an App Group. A macOS bundle or profile that carries it is a portal or build-setting drift finding for TASK-260715-3jloqy or TASK-260715-uyju7n, never a local exception
verification.assertions.6.text	LEAST PRIVILEGE (rule K2) — keychain-access-groups is ABSENT from every macOS bundle on every channel, production and probe alike. The macOS host reads and writes its own Data Protection Keychain items through its default access group 262RZ595FP.works.relux.tunnel.mac, which the injected com.apple.application-identifier entitlement provides, so a macOS bundle declaring keychain-access-groups is a build-setting drift finding for TASK-260715-uyju7n or a portal finding for TASK-260715-3jloqy, never a local exception. Asserted against the signed BUNDLE only: a Mac Development profile's team-wide keychain-access-groups = ['<TEAMID>.*'] wildcard is a profile property and is not a finding
verification.assertions.7.text	LEAST PRIVILEGE (rule G4) — com.apple.security.application-groups is ABSENT from both iOS probe bundles and no probe profile names an App Group. The iOS probe's host-to-provider exchange is NETunnelProviderSession.sendProviderMessage (TASK-260715-1jckn0 AC3), which needs no group; the probe App Group record was deleted in r5. This is the negative half of that task's AC5 archive inspection
verification.assertions.8.text	keychain-access-groups present only where status == required, which after r5 is ios.host and ios.provider alone; ABSENT from every macOS target, production and probe alike, and from the iOS probe pair. r5, rule K2: macos.host lost the group because no second macOS target reads it, so a macOS bundle carrying the key is drift for TASK-260715-uyju7n or TASK-260715-3jloqy, never a local exception
verification.assertions.20.text	production and probe App Groups are disjoint
c1AuthorizationScope.explicitlyNotAuthorized.7	any App Group record — group.works.relux.tunnel is deferred to the iOS sitting under ADR-024, and the probe record group.works.relux.tunnel.probe was deleted in r5 and must not be created at all
c1AuthorizationScope.appGroupNote	r5: Ceremony C1 creates NO App Group record and enables the App Groups capability on NO App ID. Rule G4 prohibits com.apple.security.application-groups on every macOS target, so no C1-authorized App ID needs it. Only ONE App Group record now exists in this contract, group.works.relux.tunnel, whose consumers are the two production iOS App IDs that C1 explicitly does not authorize; it is allocated when iOS re-arms under ADR-024. The probe record was deleted in r5 when the iOS probe pair lost the entitlement (verdict 04 F3).
appGroupLeastPrivilegeRule.probeRule	r5, reviewer verdict 04 F3. A probe inherits identifiers and profile classes from the production shape it rehearses, but it does NOT inherit entitlements. Each probe row is tested against the probe task's own scope and acceptance criteria. TASK-260715-1jckn0 (iOS) and TASK-260715-1r0fxv (macOS) both scope their probe to the NETunnelProviderManager lifecycle plus a versioned app message, and neither names a shared container, so no probe target on either platform is granted an App Group. If a probe is meant to prove that a capability can be provisioned at all, that is a VERIFICATION deliverable and must be written into the probe task as an acceptance criterion first; the entitlement then follows the criterion, not the other way round.
downstreamConsequences.0.consequence	Ceremony C1 shrinks, and its board text was corrected in r5 to match this scope (verdict 04 F2). The sitting authorizes exactly four macOS App IDs - works.relux.tunnel.mac, works.relux.tunnel.mac.tunnel, works.relux.tunnel.probe.mac, works.relux.tunnel.probe.mac.tunnel - the Network Extensions capability on those four, and one Mac Development profile each with this Mac registered. It creates NO App Group record, enables the App Groups capability on NO App ID, and performs NO Keychain access group mutation (there is no such portal record). All four iOS App IDs are explicitly excluded under ADR-024. Fewer portal mutations, same single sitting.
downstreamConsequences.3.consequence	The macOS probe pair carries no App Group and no keychain group. Its entitlement inspection (its AC5) gains A16 and A17 as negative checks. The probe's app-message path is unaffected - sendProviderMessage needs no App Group and no keychain group.
downstreamConsequences.7.consequence	r5, verdict 04 F3: the iOS probe pair carries NO App Group and NO keychain group. Its host-to-provider exchange is NETunnelProviderSession.sendProviderMessage only, which is exactly what its AC3 already specifies, so no acceptance criterion changes and no scope is added. Its AC5 archive inspection gains assertion A18 as a negative check: neither probe bundle may declare com.apple.security.application-groups. If this task ever gains a real shared-container deliverable, that is the amendment path in the probe rows' reopensOnly - the acceptance criterion is written first and the entitlement follows it.
keychainLeastPrivilegeRule.settledByDecisionNotOmission	Every withheld keychain row states its own reopening condition, on both platforms and in both families. r1-r4 left the four probe rows prohibited with no such condition, which made them settled by omission - indistinguishable from an oversight, and reopenable by anyone who read the silence as 'nobody considered it'. Rule R31 now rejects a withheld row that states no reopening condition, which is the same discipline rule G4 already applies to App Groups.

