revisionLog.0.summary	Two least-privilege corrections and one board-metadata correction. F1: keychain-access-groups is now PROHIBITED on macos.host and therefore on all four macOS targets. New rule K2 states the test K1 did not cover - the entitlement's only function is sharing, and the macOS host has no second member to share with, because rule K1 excludes the root/system-domain provider and neither probe touches the Data Protection Keychain. The host keeps full access to its own items through the default access group the injected com.apple.application-identifier provides. K2 also records the ordering law that makes withholding safe and its one real consequence: the macOS default group moves from the shared literal to the app-ID literal, which TASK-260715-379cpk must be authored against. F3: com.apple.security.application-groups is now PROHIBITED on ios.probe.host and ios.probe.provider, because TASK-260715-1jckn0 scopes the probe to the NETunnelProviderManager lifecycle and versioned app messaging and names no shared container; G4 gains an explicit probeRule saying a probe inherits identifiers and profile classes but never entitlements. That left group.works.relux.tunnel.probe with no consumer, so the record is DELETED and G3 is restated as the stronger property that no probe carries a group at all. One App Group record remains. F2: TASK-260728-q5kjta's scope and AC4 were corrected on the board to name the same four macOS App IDs, Network Extensions only, zero App Group records and capabilities, no Keychain portal mutation, the Mac Development profile class, and all four excluded iOS App IDs. Every r2/r3/r4 decision the reviewer listed as acceptable is preserved.
revisionLog.0.verifiedIndependently	Both findings were re-checked before being accepted. F1: the claim that withholding costs the host nothing was verified against Apple's own text rather than assumed. 'Sharing access to keychain items among a collection of apps' states that the application identifier entitlement is added to every app at signing, that the system recognizes it as the app's default keychain access group, that items stored in it are private to the app, and that 'if you don't specify any keychain access groups, then the app ID is the default'. TN3137 states that macOS builds the access-group list from code signing entitlements authorized by a provisioning profile, and macos.host is provisioned on both channels, so the default group is available. The same source also yields the one genuine consequence the reviewer did not name: the default group MOVES when the entitlement is withdrawn, which is now recorded as a downstream requirement rather than discovered at first build. F3: TASK-260715-1jckn0's scope and acceptance criteria were read in full; they name NETunnelProviderManager save/load/enable/start/status/app-message/stop, one versioned provider response, and clean stop, and no shared container, UserDefaults suite, or snapshot anywhere. The r4 rationale's claim that the probe exercises the production shared-container path is not supported by that contract. BOTH CONFIRMED.
revisionLog.1.summary	com.apple.security.application-groups is now PROHIBITED on all four macOS targets and required on iOS only (new rule G4). The macOS credential transport question is closed: TASK-260728-7ii1xz selected the file-based system-domain keychain seeded over the NE app-message channel, with the non-secret snapshot travelling in providerConfiguration, and rejected the XPC candidate. None of the six grants the App Group entitlement actually confers on macOS is used by that design, so the capability has no current function on any macOS target and is withheld. Ceremony C1 no longer creates any App Group record or enables the App Groups capability on any macOS App ID. keychain-access-groups on macos.provider moves from prohibited-pending-decision to a settled prohibited, and loses the resolutionOwner and amendmentRule that existed only to keep it open; rule K1 replaces that amendmentRule with the correct, narrow reopening condition. OC-1 is closed; OC-4 is downgraded. Every accepted r2/r3 decision - the four production identifiers and their containment, channel-keyed Network Extension values, the registered iOS-style App Group literals, the legacy no-migration rule, the Sparkle Mach lookup exception and rule X1, profile classes, and C1's iOS exclusions - is preserved.
revisionLog.2.summary	The macos.host entitlement set now carries com.apple.security.temporary-exception.mach-lookup.global-name with Sparkle's $(PRODUCT_BUNDLE_IDENTIFIER)-spks / -spki values, attributed to project-architecture and owned by TASK-260715-uyju7n. Rule X1 adds a reviewed-exception register and an explicit prohibited set for Hardened Runtime relaxations, which is the same spec clause the finding exposed. Assertions A10e, A14 and A15 make all of it machine-checkable, and the r2 assertion set is pinned so it cannot silently shrink again. Every r2 decision — four production identifiers and containment, channel-keyed Network Extension values, identical registered iOS-style App Group literals, Keychain withholding on macos.provider, the legacy no-migration rule, C1 scope, the gap task, and dependencies — is preserved byte-for-byte.
appGroupPurposeRule.macOS	No function, therefore no grant. The provider runs as root, so its group container resolves under /private/var/root while the host's resolves under the logged-in user's home; the two never meet. The macOS host-to-provider channel selected by TASK-260728-7ii1xz is providerConfiguration for non-secret state plus a sendProviderMessage seed into a provider-owned system-domain keychain item, and the XPC candidate that would have needed a group-prefixed Mach name was rejected. com.apple.security.application-groups is prohibited on all four macOS targets under rule G4.
keychainAccessGroups.0.scopeNote	iOS only since r5. r1-r4 also placed this group on macos.host; rule K2 withdrew it because no second macOS target reads it. The record itself survives because the two iOS targets that consume it are a genuine sharing pair - the host writes the credential and the appex reads it in the same user context. There is still no portal record to create: keychain sharing is authorized by the profile's team-wide keychain-access-groups wildcard, as portalNote records.
