crossPlatformRules.1	One App Group RECORD per family, and its iOS-style literal is identical on both platforms (rule G1) - but the ENTITLEMENT is granted on iOS only (rule G4). On macOS the root provider and the user-context host resolve different containers and the selected transport uses no App Group mechanism, so all four macOS targets are prohibited. The records themselves stay defined here so the iOS row re-arms unchanged.
crossPlatformRules.2	One Keychain access group NAME, granted only where it is functional (rule K1): the iOS host and appex, and the macOS host. Never the macOS provider, which reads the system-domain keychain, where access groups do not apply.
crossPlatformRules.4	The Sparkle Mach lookup exception is NOT shared at all (rule X1). It is granted to macos.host alone, because Sparkle is linked into that target alone; iOS updates through the App Store and no provider updates anything.
verification.assertions.3.text	com.apple.developer.system-extension.install present on macOS hosts only
verification.assertions.6.text	LEAST PRIVILEGE (rule K2) — keychain-access-groups is ABSENT from every macOS bundle on every channel, production and probe alike. The macOS host reads and writes its own Data Protection Keychain items through its default access group 262RZ595FP.works.relux.tunnel.mac, which the injected com.apple.application-identifier entitlement provides, so a macOS bundle declaring keychain-access-groups is a build-setting drift finding for TASK-260715-uyju7n or a portal finding for TASK-260715-3jloqy, never a local exception. Asserted against the signed BUNDLE only: a Mac Development profile's team-wide keychain-access-groups = ['<TEAMID>.*'] wildcard is a profile property and is not a finding
verification.assertions.8.text	keychain-access-groups present only where status == required, which after r5 is ios.host and ios.provider alone; ABSENT from every macOS target, production and probe alike, and from the iOS probe pair. r5, rule K2: macos.host lost the group because no second macOS target reads it, so a macOS bundle carrying the key is drift for TASK-260715-uyju7n or TASK-260715-3jloqy, never a local exception
verification.assertions.17.text	com.apple.security.temporary-exception.mach-lookup.global-name is present on the macOS host bundle with exactly the two values in entitlements[key].resolvedValue — <host-bundle-id>-spks then <host-bundle-id>-spki — and is ABSENT from the macOS provider, from every iOS bundle, and from every probe bundle. The authored form uses $(PRODUCT_BUNDLE_IDENTIFIER); a signed bundle carries the resolved form, so compare against resolvedValue, not value
openConstraints.0.statement	The macOS packet-tunnel system extension runs as root in a non-user context. The Data Protection Keychain is available only to code running in a user context, and keychain access groups share items between programs running as the SAME user. The .spec/security-privacy.md design — private keys and passphrases in the Data Protection Keychain shared through one access group with the packet tunnel extension — therefore CANNOT work between the macOS host and the macOS provider. It works unchanged on iOS.
openConstraints.0.corroboration	Observed 2026-07-28 on this Mac. (a) Two shipping Developer ID packet-tunnel system extensions ship com.apple.security.temporary-exception.files.absolute-path.read-write entries for /private/var/root/Library/Group Containers/ (Tailscale, Surfshark) — direct evidence that a root provider's group container is not the user's. (b) Surfshark's provider carries a temporary-exception path for /Library/Keychains/, i.e. the FILE-BASED System keychain, not the Data Protection Keychain. (c) PureVPN's providers carry keychain-access-groups outright. Together these indicate the real-world macOS pattern is a file-based/System keychain or an IPC seed, not a shared Data Protection Keychain access group.
openConstraints.0.resolution	Confirmed as stated and then resolved: the .spec/security-privacy.md shared Data-Protection-Keychain-access-group design does not work between the macOS host and a root provider, and works unchanged on iOS. The replacement is candidate D - the provider owns a file-based system-domain keychain item, seeded once from the host over NETunnelProviderSession.sendProviderMessage. Matrix consequence: macos.provider keychain-access-groups is settled prohibited (rule K1), not pending. Downstream tasks 379cpk, 1o9wjz, 29ws8l, 2hhh7x and 3f4lxy each revise the assumption named in TASK-260728-7ii1xz_macos-credential-transport-decision.md section 8.
openConstraints.3.statement	If a sandboxed XPC Mach service is EVER selected as the macOS host-to-provider channel, the endpoint name must be prefixed by an App Group ID the sandboxed process is entitled to. TASK-260728-7ii1xz evaluated that channel as candidate C and rejected it, and rule G4 now prohibits com.apple.security.application-groups on every macOS target, so no macOS target holds a group prefix today and the question is dormant rather than pending. Every Apple example of the pattern found, and all three shipping products inspected on this Mac, use a macOS-style Team-ID-prefixed group for the prefix - and all of those predate the Feb 2025 change.
c1AuthorizationScope.keychainNote	r5: C1 authorizes NO Keychain access group mutation, and never did require one - keychain sharing has no portal record (keychainAccessGroups[].portalNote). Rule K2 additionally removes the group from macos.host, so no C1-authorized App ID declares keychain-access-groups at all. An operator asked to 'create the Keychain access group' at C1 is being asked for a mutation that does not exist.
appGroupLeastPrivilegeRule.reopensOnly	This row reopens if, and only if, one of three things becomes true, each of which is a grant in the survey above rather than a general appeal to App Groups: (a) the macOS host-to-provider channel becomes a group-prefixed Mach service, i.e. the XPC candidate is revived; (b) it becomes a group-prefixed POSIX IPC object; or (c) the provider is moved out of root into a user context, which would make the group container genuinely shared. It does NOT reopen because a spec sentence mentions App Groups, because a shipping third-party product carries the key, or to spare a portal sitting. Reopening is an amendment under section 11, naming which of (a), (b) or (c) became true.
keychainScopeRule.hostScope	K1 governs the macOS PROVIDER only. It says nothing about the macOS host, which does run in a user context and does use the Data Protection Keychain, and that silence is what let r4 keep a shared access group on macos.host with no second member to share with. Rule K2 closes it: sharing is the entitlement's only function, so a host with no co-member is served by its default access group. Reviewer verdict 04 F1.
downstreamConsequences.0.consequence	Ceremony C1 shrinks, and its board text was corrected in r5 to match this scope (verdict 04 F2). The sitting authorizes exactly four macOS App IDs - works.relux.tunnel.mac, works.relux.tunnel.mac.tunnel, works.relux.tunnel.probe.mac, works.relux.tunnel.probe.mac.tunnel - the Network Extensions capability on those four, and one Mac Development profile each with this Mac registered. It creates NO App Group record, enables the App Groups capability on NO App ID, and performs NO Keychain access group mutation (there is no such portal record). All four iOS App IDs are explicitly excluded under ADR-024. Fewer portal mutations, same single sitting.
downstreamConsequences.2.consequence	Generated macOS targets must declare NEITHER com.apple.security.application-groups NOR keychain-access-groups, and neither the App Groups nor the Keychain Sharing capability may be added to them in Xcode. G1's instruction about the Register App Groups build setting now applies to iOS targets only. A macOS .entitlements file carrying either key fails A16 or A17. The macos.host development allowlist is consequently five authored keys plus three injected, and seven keys on developer-id (section 9.1).
downstreamConsequences.8.consequence	r5, verdict 04 F1: the macOS Keychain vault must NOT pass kSecAttrAccessGroup. On macOS the host's items live in its default access group, 262RZ595FP.works.relux.tunnel.mac, which the injected com.apple.application-identifier provides; naming the shared group would return errSecMissingEntitlement because macos.host no longer declares keychain-access-groups. On iOS the vault continues to name 262RZ595FP.works.relux.tunnel.shared explicitly, because there the group is real and shared with the appex. This is a platform branch in the vault, not a shared constant. Note that the macOS default group MOVED in r5 (rule K2 defaultAccessGroupLaw): under r4 an unqualified macOS write would have landed in the shared group, under r5 it lands in the app-ID group. No item has been written yet, so there is nothing to migrate - but the vault must not be authored against the r4 default.
downstreamConsequences.9.consequence	r5: unchanged in substance and now unambiguous. The macOS extension credential resolver reads the provider-owned file-based system-domain keychain item seeded over the app-message channel (TASK-260728-7ii1xz candidate D); it never reads a Data Protection Keychain access group, and after r5 there is no macOS access group for it to read. On iOS the resolver reads 262RZ595FP.works.relux.tunnel.shared as before.
keychainLeastPrivilegeRule.statement	keychain-access-groups is granted only to a target that must share a Data Protection Keychain item with a NAMED second target in this contract. A target that only reads and writes its own items is already served by the default access group the signing toolchain gives it, so the entitlement would add signed authority with no consumer. On iOS the host and the appex are two such members and both are granted. On macOS there is no second member today, so the key is prohibited on all four macOS targets.
keychainLeastPrivilegeRule.defaultAccessGroupLaw	The system forms each app's access-group array as the keychain-access-groups entitlement array first, then the application identifier, then the app groups; the FIRST element is the default group that keychain services uses when a caller names none. Two consequences this contract depends on. (a) A target with no keychain-access-groups entitlement still has a private default group equal to <TeamID>.<bundle id>, so withholding never costs a target access to its own items. (b) Withholding MOVES the default: macos.host's default group changes from 262RZ595FP.works.relux.tunnel.shared under r4 to 262RZ595FP.works.relux.tunnel.mac under r5. A consumer must not paper that over with an explicit kSecAttrAccessGroup naming the shared group - on macOS the correct call names no access group at all, and naming the shared one returns errSecMissingEntitlement.
keychainLeastPrivilegeRule.relationToK1	K1 explains why the macOS PROVIDER can never be a member: the file-based system-domain keychain it uses has no access groups. K2 is the rule K1 did not cover - why the macOS HOST, which does use the Data Protection Keychain, still gets no custom group. Reviewer verdict 04 F1 named exactly that gap: the provider-side test was applied and the host-side one was not.

